-- Relocating and dispatching in a body that cannot see the type it is doing either to.
--
-- Compiled with specialization declined (see the `.generic` marker), so what runs is the erased
-- form: the size, the relocation and the witnesses all come out of the environment the caller built.
--
-- Two things are asserted here that nothing else asserts:
--
--  - a `->` of a type variable relocates through the `moveInit` slot of the descriptor rather than
--    by a block copy of a size this body does not have. Read back field by field, since a
--    relocation that went missing leaves the destination holding whatever the copy put there.
--    ErasedRelocate.yana asserts the same property on the JavaScript target, where the slot answers
--    a shape rather than a size.
--  - a dispatch on a requirement another one implies goes through the witness the caller already
--    passed. `Num(a)` is what these signatures declare; `FromInt(a)` is what a literal needs, and it
--    is reached through `Num`'s own superclass pointer rather than through a second slot.
--
-- This file used to be `ErasedSink.yana`, and the relocation half was asserted by a counter an
-- authored `Sink` instance incremented. That class is gone - doc/spec/core.md records why - so the
-- half that remains unique to this fixture is the witness one, and the name says so.

import Native

data Handle {value: I64}

-- A nested record, so the relocation carries a member that is itself an aggregate.
data Pair {left: Handle, right: I64}

-- The relocation the body cannot name. `let ->y = x` is the one consumer of a move with no
-- destination of its own, so it provides one - and filling it is a call through the descriptor.
fn relocate(->x: a) -> a:
    let ->y = x
    return y

-- The implied requirement, dispatched from a body that declared only what implies it.
fn (Num(a)) increment(x: a) -> a = x + 1

-- And the same, one call further out: `twice` has `Num(a)` in hand and `increment` wants a witness
-- for it, so this call site forwards its own slot rather than building anything.
fn (Num(a)) twice(x: a) -> a = increment(increment(x))

-- The forwarded case of the same relation: what `unit` declares is what `numeric` only implies, so
-- the environment `numeric` builds for the call fills that slot by stepping through the `Num`
-- witness it was handed rather than by asking its own caller for a second one.
fn (FromInt(a)) unit(like: a) -> a = 1

fn (Num(a)) numeric(x: a) -> a = unit(x)

fn main() -> I64:
    let h = Handle {value: 10}
    let moved = relocate(h)
    if moved.value != 10 then return -1

    let p = Pair {left: Handle {value: 20}, right: 5}
    let movedPair = relocate(p)
    if movedPair.left.value != 20 then return -2
    if movedPair.right != 5 then return -3

    -- 10 + 20 + 5 + 21 + 1 = 57, where 21 is `twice(19)` and the 1 is `numeric`'s literal, built
    -- through a witness it never asked its caller for.
    return moved.value + movedPair.left.value + movedPair.right + twice(19) + numeric(4)
